        "opportunities": [
          {
            "idea": "Carry install identity forward in the full re-overlay path, the same way the delta path does. In InstallModOnlyAsync, take ClonedAoe3 and Aoe3SourcePath from the previousManifest it already loads (using previous?.ClonedAoe3 ?? false). Take the install label from previous.AppName/ProductGuid, or from ModState.ActiveInstallLabel, and use it for shortcuts, registry entries and WriteManifest. Do the same in ApplyGitHubDeltaAsync.",
            "reusableCode": "NativeInstallService.cs:582 (previousManifest), 794-797 (delta carry-forward pattern), 2315-2328 (AppNameFor/ProductGuidFor), 2621-2647 (WriteManifest already carries user-data fields); UninstallSafetyTests, MultiInstallPhase1Tests",
            "risks": "An in-place first install must still write false; previous?.ClonedAoe3 ?? false does that. Old builds stamped true on every install, but UninstallService's IsAoe3Root veto still covers them (CLAUDE.md:1000-1011). The label has to come from the manifest, not the renamable UI label, or an existing Add/Remove key could be orphaned.",
            "appliesToAllMods": "Every IsolatedFolder and InPlaceOverlay mod, current and future, under both mechanisms that write manifests (GitHubReleases, WolPatcher). The stock game never gets here."
          },
          {
            "idea": "Add one pure predicate, RepairEligibility(profile, manifest, config), returning Allowed or Refused(reason) and the payload source. The menu, the Properties dialog, Verify Retry and RepairInstallAsync itself would all call it. It would check IsStockGame, the mechanism (WolPatcher/GitHubReleases, or a future declared install payload) and manifest ownership (ManifestClaimsAnotherMod with previousIds). It would also apply the global payloadZipUrls/InstallerZipUrl override only to the WoL built-in.",
            "reusableCode": "ModInstallProbe.ManifestClaimsAnotherMod (ModInstallProbe.cs:272-293), UninstallService.ShouldRemoveOverlayOnly pattern (pure, tested), MainWindow.CanResolvePayload (10396-10418)",
            "risks": "Scoping the override to WoL changes behavior for anyone with a custom mirror on a non-WoL mod, which is almost certainly never intentional. The Properties Repair button would become disabled for DelegatedExternal/Manual mods, a visible change.",
            "appliesToAllMods": "All mods. It is keyed on declared shape, not mod id. A future mechanism string falls back to Manual, so it is refused cleanly."
          },
          {
            "idea": "Split what a repair re-lays from what an update lays. A plain repair re-lays the INSTALLED version (manifest.Version / LastKnownVersion), respects PinnedVersion, and never downgrades. The existing tail (CheckAsync, then pending update or auto-continue) keeps the 'repair plus updates' behavior as a separate, visible step.",
            "reusableCode": "ResolveInstallVersion/EffectiveGitHubTag (MainWindow.xaml.cs:10425-10456), UpdateService.ResolveEffectiveGitHubTag (pure, tested in GitHubFollowLatestTests), the tail auto-continue (8573-8592), MaybeAutoContinueUpdateAfterInstall (11565-11595)",
            "risks": "This departs from the maintainer's 'reinstall everything together with updates' model (CLAUDE.md:697-699), so it needs their sign-off. External-hosted mods can only re-lay the approved tag (SHA-pinned). If the installed tag is no longer on GitHub, fall back to the effective tag and say so. WolPatcher has only one payload snapshot, so this applies to GitHubReleases only.",
            "appliesToAllMods": "GitHubReleases (all 4 current catalog mods, and future ones). WolPatcher keeps its snapshot-then-patch model. DelegatedExternal, Manual and the stock game are out of scope."
          },
          {
            "idea": "Give the plain-repair damaged branch the same patch-only rescue the update branch has. Fetch the release graph when the target has no full zip, rebuild from BaselinePolicy.Any, then patch up to the installed or target tag.",
            "reusableCode": "MainWindow.xaml.cs:8298-8369 (rescue block), DeltaChainPlanner.Build/BaselineUrlsOf, GitHubReleaseDownloader.PickAssetIndex/ListReleaseGraphAsync",
            "risks": "It costs one extra rate-limited API call, but only when a full zip is missing. It must stamp the version actually laid down (the actuallyLaidDown pattern) and must never patch over the damaged install itself.",
            "appliesToAllMods": "GitHubReleases mods with deltaPatches (none today, supported by the schema). Nothing changes for any other mod."
          },
          {
            "idea": "Always run an idempotent step that re-asserts state outside the payload, even when the install is intact. That covers ApplyPrivateSetupPath (key plus exe patch, idempotent), the user-data seed (already done), and a translation reconcile or re-apply after any overlay re-lay. Run the addon re-apply only when overlay files were actually re-laid, and back up only files still byte-identical to what the manifest recorded.",
            "reusableCode": "NativeInstallService.ApplyPrivateSetupPath (2535-2578; would need to be internal), SetupPathPatcher.IsPrivateKeyCurrent/EnsurePrivateKey, TranslationService.ReconcileAfterUpdate (373-413), UserDataPayloadService.ApplyAsync pattern (287-291), AddonService.ReapplyAllAsync (300-344)",
            "risks": "The private key needs admin. Check IsPrivateKeyCurrent first so an intact repair asks for elevation only when the key is actually broken. Changing the addon re-apply must keep the stale-backup guard for a real re-overlay (addons.md).",
            "appliesToAllMods": "Any mod that declares privateSetupPath, translations, userDataPayload or has addons enabled. Each step is a no-op for mods that don't declare it, so the change is generic."
          },
          {
            "idea": "Add base-file (clone) repair for IsolatedFolder installs. Record fingerprints for exes that come from the clone (the GameExecutable when it is not in the payload, plus EngineCandidates). When verify finds one damaged, re-copy it from manifest.Aoe3SourcePath, provided that folder is still a clean AoE3 (AoE3Detector.IsCleanAoE3Folder). Then re-apply privateSetupPath, and never restore files listed in CloneFilesRemovedByPatches or superseded XMBs.",
            "reusableCode": "InstallManifest.EngineFileHashes/Aoe3SourcePath, VerifyService.VerifyEngineFiles, NativeInstallService.SelectCloneFilesToRemove/SelectSupersededCompiledXml (to exclude), FolderCloneService",
            "risks": "It depends on the clonedAoe3/Aoe3SourcePath carry-forward fix landing first. The source AoE3 may have been patched, re-languaged or moved since the install, so compare against the recorded hash before copying. It must never run for InPlaceOverlay, where the folder is the source.",
            "appliesToAllMods": "IsolatedFolder mods only (all current ones). Explicitly excluded: InPlaceOverlay, the stock game, DelegatedExternal and Manual."
          },
          {
            "idea": "Stop using WoL-only checks for any WolPatcher profile: key the zulushield/AI3/bar/sound verify layer on profile.InstallMarker (or IsBuiltIn(wol)). Then close the community WolPatcher gaps: project payloadSha256, or state in MODDING.md that WolPatcher is first-party only.",
            "reusableCode": "MainWindow.VerifyInstallation (10152-10215), ModRegistry.ProjectToProfile (703-712), TryAcceptPayloadOverride (520-544)",
            "risks": "Projecting payloadSha256 changes the catalog-cache shape only if a new field is added, which would need a CacheSchemaVersion bump. Keying on the marker must keep WoL's existing checks exactly as they are.",
            "appliesToAllMods": "Only WolPatcher-shaped mods, meaning WoL today and any future legacy-pipeline mod. Nothing changes for GitHubReleases."
          }
        ],
        "openQuestions": [
          "Is repair-as-update (re-lay the effective or latest tag) the maintainer's deliberate model ('reinstale todo junto con las actualizaciones', CLAUDE.md:697-699), and if so is breaking the pin or downgrading when the followLatest cache is empty acceptable? This decides the 'repair the installed version' opportunity.",
          "For WolPatcher, ResolveInstallVersion stamps CurrentVersion ?? LatestVersion while laying the fixed 1.2.0e payload snapshot. Once WoL ships a patch beyond the payload, would the manifest's Version and KeyFileHashes name a version the bytes don't match, so the post-repair CheckAsync finds no pending patches? Dormant today because payload == latest; not traced end-to-end.",
          "Should DelegatedExternal and Manual mods ever be repairable, for example if install.payloadUrls/payloadSha256 were finally consumed? Or should every repair entry point hide Repair for them?",
          "Does InPlaceOverlay back up base files it overwrites, as MODDING §4.2 says, or not, as CLAUDE.md:997-999 says? I did not read CopyPayloadToDestinationAsync. It matters for repair on an in-place mod.",
          "Do any current GitHubReleases mods publish split (.zip.001…) or patch-only releases? This needs the GitHub API, which I did not call. Nothing in the catalog declares it.",
          "K&B userDataRedirect: the template (aoe3-mods-catalog-template/mods/knights-and-barbarians-remastered/mod.json) says false; the live catalog and the local catalog repo say true. Which is intended?",
          "Does EnsureGameNotRunning (GameProcessName) match by exe name only? If so, two mods with the same exe (K&B's age3k.exe, King's Return's age3k.exe) could close the wrong mod's game before a repair.",
          "Several docs have drifted from the code: ModCatalogManifest's doc-comment references a ModCatalogManifestValidator that doesn't exist, MODDING.md §6.2 says the WoL SHA isn't enforced, and MODDING §5.3 says DelegatedExternal 'installs the initial payload' when no payload source exists for it. Should these be fixed together with the repair work?"
        ]
      },
      {
        "area": "ux-tests-history",
        "summary": "ENTRY POINTS. Repair and Verify are two buttons in Mod Properties > LOCAL FILES. Repair is a ghost button on the ACTIVE COPY card (ModPropertiesDialog.xaml:626-628). Verify is the solid button in the \"Something not working?\" card (ModPropertiesDialog.xaml:664-667). Both close the dialog first (ModPropertiesDialog.xaml.cs:2080-2090), then call RaiseMenuClick on the hidden legacy gear items (MainWindow.xaml.cs:5363-5364, 6574-6578). Those land in MenuRepairInstall_Click, which refuses the stock game and uninstalled mods (MainWindow.xaml.cs:9836-9848), and MenuVerifyFiles_Click, which leads to VerifyButton_Click (9850-9855). The gear icon itself only opens the Properties dialog (6003-6008). For the stock game both buttons are hidden (ModPropertiesDialog.xaml.cs:567-583). The dialog enables Repair whenever the mod is installed (554) and does not check that the launcher can actually install the mod (the gear item does, at MainWindow.xaml.cs:13481-13484). For a DelegatedExternal or Manual mod, the button therefore closes the dialog and reports \"no install URL\" (CanResolvePayload at 10396-10418).\n\nVERIFY FLOW (VerifyButton_Click, 7677-7789). It is cancellable and runs VerifyInstallation(hashPass:true) (10102-10298), which has three layers:\n- Structural checks: the probe file, the 3 AoE3 base key files, and the WoL-only folders and .bar files.\n- A per-file SHA-256 check against manifest.FileHashes (VerifyService.VerifyAgainstManifest).\n- Engine files, reported with a \"reinstall AoE3\" suffix (10246-10253). Unexpected extra files go to the log only (10255-10267).\nIf there are problems, the file names only go to SetStatus as a count plus the first 10 names (7763-7764) and to the log (7765-7766). ShowProgressError then shows DlgVerifyRepairBody, and a \"Retry\" button runs RepairInstallAsync() (7702, 7770).\n\nKEY UX FINDING (verified). ProgressPanelControl lives inside LegacyPlayContent, which is Visibility=\"Collapsed\" (MainWindow.xaml:1596-1611; hidden since v0.9.4, commit 7f819bc on 2026-05-25). The visible dashboard strip copies only the title, the step text, speed, ETA, percent, the bar, and Pause/Cancel (SyncDashboardProgressFromLegacyPanel, 4144-4206). It never copies ProgressMessageText or ProgressActionRetry. The status TextBlock is also a collapsed stub (Controls/MainTabs.xaml:37-42). As a result:\n- The user sees \"Operation failed\" with no explanation for 10 seconds (ErrorHoldSeconds, 9696).\n- After that the idle subtitle shows the \"N problems: a, b, c\" line.\n- The Verify→Repair handoff (the Retry button) is not visible anywhere.\n\nREPAIR FLOW (RepairInstallAsync, 8006-8593). The same method also runs GitHubReleases updates, version picks and auto-continue (callers at 7644, 10481, 11593, 11913). The steps:\n1. A plain repair (plainRepair = !asUpdate && targetReleaseTag == null, line 8158) whose manifest has FileHashes first runs VerifyAgainstManifest, covering the overlay only (8170-8189).\n2. If intact: set `intact` and fall through with no download (8190-8197).\n3. If damaged: EnsureInstallWritableOrElevate (8205), then ConfirmRepairSpaceOk (8207), then InstallModOnlyAsync, which re-lays the whole overlay (8212-8225).\n4. Any other case (update, version pick, or a manifest without hashes) always writes: elevation, then delta chain or baseline rescue or full re-overlay (8228-8390).\n5. Recheck: skipped when intact (8394-8398); otherwise structural only, hashPass:false (8399-8409). With hashPass false, VerifyInstallation falls to the random 200-file zero-byte spot-check (10269-10295).\n6. Then addon re-apply (8418), the user-data seed (8423), stamping LastKnownVersion (8440-8465), the bell (8483), and the status message.\n7. Catch blocks: cancel, SetupPath*, and a generic Exception (8505-8530). There is NO PayloadFileBlockedException handler.\n8. Tail: a translation reconcile runs only when asUpdate && updated (8540-8567). Otherwise CheckAsync, then continue into pending WoL patches (8573-8591).\nSupporting pieces: disk space uses a fixed 3 GiB temp allowance plus 1 GiB install headroom (7490-7502; DiskSpaceService.cs:40,49). Elevation relaunches with NO resume argument (8651), unlike the update path, which passes --update-now (8739).\n\nGAME EXIT. OnGameExited (12281-12406) calls WarnGameClosedImmediately when the game dies within 8 s (12269, 12403). That runs an existence-only sweep, InstallSnapshot.CountMissingFiles (12513-12543; InstallSnapshot.cs:257-273), and shows one of two toasts. ToastGameClosedImmediatelyBody tells the user to use Verify or Repair \"from the gear menu\". ToastGameClosedImmediatelyBodyFilesOk says \"Your files are fine\". Neither toast has an action button (12537).\n\nANTIVIRUS. PayloadFileBlockedException is thrown in three places (NativeInstallService.cs):\n- By VerifyExtractIntact (1056-1069).\n- On ERROR_VIRUS_INFECTED/DELETED during the staged extract (1138-1148).\n- During the direct extract (1506-1511, then 1545).\nInstallAsync catches it and opens AntivirusExclusionDialog.ShowBlocked (11411-11442). It also shows the preventive notice before downloading, but only for profile.AntivirusFalsePositiveFile (10852-10867), which is set only by the built-in WoL profile (ModRegistry.cs:979) and is not projected from catalog mod.json. RepairInstallAsync has neither. The loop is therefore real: Defender quarantines a file, Verify says \"missing\", Repair re-downloads the whole payload (the combined zip is deleted at the start of every run, NativeInstallService.cs:887), Defender deletes the file again, VerifyExtractIntact throws, the generic catch shows the raw English exception text on the hidden panel, and Retry repeats the multi-GB download. No exclusion dialog appears.\n\nHISTORY. Granular repair was added in commit 64bd61d (2026-06-24, message \".\"). It introduced NativeInstallService.RepairFilesAsync plus the whole verify system. It was documented in c11b79c (2026-06-25, \".\"), and removed 2 days later in 8767ab2 (2026-06-26, message \"-\"). The commit messages carry no rationale. The only stated reason is the CLAUDE.md diff made in the same commit: 'the maintainer's explicit model: \"reinstale todo junto con las actualizaciones\"' (\"reinstall everything, together with the updates\"). The same diff also records three things:\n- The intact path must NOT `return` (the granular version did, which skipped the pending-update continuation).\n- Repair must lay down a complete, known-good state and rewrite the manifest (RepairFilesAsync deliberately did NOT rewrite the manifest).\n- Repair progress must use real phase handlers (the granular call passed null handlers, so the bar froze at about 60%).\nTechnical flaws visible in the removed code, which I inferred from the diffs rather than found stated:\n- It still downloaded the full ZIP, so it saved write I/O only, never download time.\n- The caller discarded its RepairResult.Unrepairable list.\n- It copied the payload's BASE bytes over files whose manifest hashes had been re-captured AFTER WoL patches (RecaptureHashes was added in the same commit 64bd61d). Any damaged file that a patch had changed was therefore \"repaired\" to the wrong version, and the subset recheck would then report it as still corrupt.\n\nTESTS. No test touches RepairInstallAsync, VerifyInstallation, InstallModOnlyAsync or WriteManifest, which all live in MainWindow code-behind or private statics. Only the extracted pure pieces are pinned; see facts.",
        "facts": [
          {
            "claim": "Granular per-file repair (NativeInstallService.RepairFilesAsync + RecordRepairResult) was introduced 2026-06-24 and removed 2026-06-26; commit messages are '.' and '-' with no rationale.",
            "evidence": "git log -S RepairFilesAsync -> 64bd61d (2026-06-24 '.'), c11b79c (2026-06-25 '.', CLAUDE.md only), 8767ab2 (2026-06-26 '-', removes 93 lines from NativeInstallService.cs), c3b74d7 (2026-09-12, AGENTS.md mention only)",
            "verified": true
          },
          {
